</head>
<body>

<!-- HERO -->
<div class="hero">
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of every Academy Award Best Original Score winner — exploring composer dominance, stylistic evolution, and historical patterns across 93 ceremonies.</p>
        <p class="meta">1934–2026 &middot; 93 Ceremonies &middot; {total_unique} Composers</p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- KPI CARDS -->
<div class="kpi-section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="kpi-grid">
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{total_unique}</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Unique Composers</div>
                <div class="kpi-detail">Individual winners across 93 ceremonies</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{max_wins_count}</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Max Wins (Single Composer)</div>
                <div class="kpi-detail">{', '.join(max_wins_names)}</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{repeat_rate:.1f}%</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Repeat Winner Rate</div>
                <div class="kpi-detail">{repeat_ceremony_count} of 93 ceremonies</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="kpi-value">{most_unique_decade}s</div>
                <div class="kpi-label">Most Diverse Decade</div>
                <div class="kpi-detail">{most_unique_decade_count} unique composers</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- SECTION 1: OVERVIEW -->
<section id="overview">
    <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">Section I</span>
        <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>
            The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been presented since the 6th Academy Awards ceremony in 1934, making it one of the longest-running categories in Oscar history. Over <span class="inline-stat">93 ceremonies</span>, the award has recognized <span class="inline-stat">{total_unique} individual composers</span> across 93 winning films — a ratio that reveals both the breadth of talent recognized and the concentration of wins among a select few.
        </p>
        <p>
            The category has evolved considerably: in its early decades, the award often went to music department heads who supervised entire scores; by the mid-century, it had shifted to recognizing individual compositional voices. The category name itself has changed multiple times, and in certain years the Academy split it into subcategories (dramatic vs. comedy/musical). This dataset consolidates to one winner per year.
        </p>
        <p>
            Of the {total_unique} individual composers who have won, <span class="inline-stat">{len(top_composers)}</span> are repeat winners (two or more wins), while <span class="inline-stat">{len(single_win_composers)}</span> won exactly once — meaning roughly {len(single_win_composers) * 100 // total_unique}% of all winning composers never returned to the podium.
        </p>
        {"<div class='data-note'>" + data_note + "</div>" if data_note else ""}
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: TOP COMPOSERS -->
<section id="top-composers">
    <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">Section II</span>
        <h2>Most Awarded Composers</h2>
        <p>Composers ranked by total Best Original Score wins. The bar chart shows all repeat winners; the table details their winning films.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <div class="chart-title">Composers with Multiple Wins</div>
        <canvas id="topComposersChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Composer</th><th>Wins</th><th>Winning Films</th><th>Span</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>'''

# Build top composers table
rank = 0
prev_count = -1
for name, count in sorted(top_composers, key=lambda x: -x[1]):
    if count != prev_count:
        rank += 1
        prev_count = count
    yrs = sorted(composer_years[name])
    films = []
    for row in rows:
        yr = int(row['Year'])
        composers_in_entry = split_composers(row['Composer'])
        if name in composers_in_entry:
            films.append(f"{row['Film']} ({row['Year']})")
    span = f"{yrs[0]}–{yrs[-1]}" if len(yrs) > 1 else str(yrs[0])
    html += f'''
                <tr>
                    <td>{rank}</td>
                    <td><strong>{name}</strong></td>
                    <td>{count}</td>
                    <td>{', '.join(films)}</td>
                    <td>{span}</td>
                </tr>'''

html += f'''
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: TEMPORAL PATTERNS -->
<section id="temporal-patterns">
    <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">Section III</span>
        <h2>Temporal Patterns &amp; Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
        <p>How the distribution of wins — and the diversity of winners — has shifted across each decade of the award's history.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">Unique Composers per Decade</div>
            <canvas id="decadeUnique"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-container">
            <div class="chart-title">First-time vs. Repeat Winners per Decade</div>
            <canvas id="decadeRepeat"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p>
            The <span class="inline-stat">{most_unique_decade}s</span> stand out as the most diverse decade, with <span class="inline-stat">{most_unique_decade_count}</span> unique individual composers winning — a reflection of the Academy's increasing openness to varied musical voices. By contrast, the 1940s and 1950s saw significant concentration, with composers like Max Steiner, Alfred Newman, Dimitri Tiomkin, and Franz Waxman collecting multiple wins.
        </p>
        <p>
            The repeat winner rate tells a compelling story of changing dynamics. In the award's first three decades (1934–1963), repeat winners were common — the same handful of studio-system composers dominated. From the 1970s onward, first-time winners became increasingly prevalent as the field diversified, though John Williams and John Barry continued to break through repeatedly.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <div class="chart-title">Complete Timeline: Ceremony Winners 1934–2026</div>
        <div class="data-table-wrapper scrollable-table">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Year</th><th>Film</th><th>Composer</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {winners_table_rows}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS -->
<section id="era-analysis">
    <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">Section IV</span>
        <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
        <p>Four broad eras reveal how the award's center of gravity has shifted — from European emigre symphonists to a globalized, genre-fluid field.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <div class="chart-title">Unique Composers by Era</div>
        <canvas id="eraChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    {era_sections_html}
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: NOTABLE MILESTONES -->
<section id="notable-milestones">
    <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">Section V</span>
        <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="milestone-grid">
        <div class="milestone-card">
            <h4>First Winner</h4>
            <p><span class="highlight">{first_winner['Composer']}</span> for <em>{first_winner['Film']}</em> ({first_winner['Year']}), at the 6th Academy Awards ceremony — the inaugural Best Original Score award.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="milestone-card">
            <h4>Most Recent Winner</h4>
            <p><span class="highlight">{last_winner['Composer']}</span> for <em>{last_winner['Film']}</em> ({last_winner['Year']}), at the 98th Academy Awards.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="milestone-card">
            <h4>Longest Gap Between Wins</h4>
            <p><span class="highlight">{longest_gap_composer}</span> went <span class="highlight">{longest_gap} years</span> between wins ({longest_gap_years[0]} → {longest_gap_years[1]}) — the longest drought before a return to the podium.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="milestone-card">
            <h4>Most Wins (Tied Record)</h4>
            <p><span class="highlight">{', '.join(max_wins_names)}</span> each hold the record at <span class="highlight">{max_wins_count} wins</span>.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="milestone-grid">
        <div class="milestone-card">
            <h4>Back-to-Back Winners</h4>
            <p>Consecutive wins by the same composer — a rare feat of sustained dominance:</p>
            <ul class="b2b-list">
                {b2b_html if b2b_html else '<li>No back-to-back winners found in the data.</li>'}
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="milestone-card">
            <h4>One-Time Winners</h4>
            <p><span class="highlight">{len(single_win_composers)}</span> of {total_unique} individual composers won exactly once — roughly <span class="highlight">{len(single_win_composers) * 100 // total_unique}%</span> of all winners. The Oscar for Best Original Score remains, for most, a once-in-a-career honor.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<section id="conclusions">
    <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">Section VI</span>
        <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>1. Concentration is giving way to diversity.</strong>
        <p>The early decades were defined by a small circle of studio-era composers winning repeatedly. Since the 2000s, the winner pool has expanded dramatically — the {most_unique_decade}s produced {most_unique_decade_count} unique composers, the most of any decade. The Academy increasingly rewards singular artistic visions over established names.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>2. A select few still tower above the field.</strong>
        <p>Despite the trend toward diversity, the all-time leaderboard remains dominated by a handful of composers. {', '.join(max_wins_names)} sit at {max_wins_count} wins each. The repeat winner rate of {repeat_rate:.1f}% shows that roughly one in four ceremonies still goes to a known quantity.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>3. Back-to-back wins mark inflection points.</strong>
        <p>Consecutive wins (Franz Waxman, Alan Menken, Gustavo Santaolalla, Ludwig Göransson among them) tend to coincide with moments when a composer's style captures the cultural moment. Menken's run paralleled Disney's animation renaissance; Göransson's reflects a new generation of genre-fluid scoring.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>4. The globalization of the award is accelerating.</strong>
        <p>From its European emigre roots through the American-dominated mid-century, the award has grown steadily more international. Recent decades have honored composers from Iceland (Hildur Guðnadóttir), India (A.R. Rahman), China (Tan Dun), Argentina (Gustavo Santaolalla), Sweden (Ludwig Göransson), and Germany (Volker Bertelmann) — a breadth unthinkable in the award's first half-century.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>5. Ludwig Göransson is the defining voice of the current era.</strong>
        <p>With wins in 2018, 2023, 2024, and 2026 (per the dataset), Göransson has assembled the most concentrated streak of wins since Alan Menken's 1989–1992 run. At 42, he is positioned to challenge the all-time record if his trajectory continues — a story worth watching as the award enters its tenth decade.</p>
    </div>
</section>

</div>

<footer>
    <div class="container">
        <p>Data source: All Oscar Soundtracks (1934–2026) &middot; Kaggle / ActarusLab &middot; Cross-referenced with AMPAS, IMDb, MusicBrainz</p>
        <p>Generated April 2026</p>
    </div>
</footer>

<script>
